# User guide (onboarding tour) must stay in sync with the UI

Flashlearn ships an in-app guided tour ("Dragon's tour") that highlights real UI
elements with a spotlight + animated arrow. It auto-shows the first time a user
visits a page and only re-shows the steps they haven't completed yet. Users can
also opt out of every tour via the "Don't show me guides again" button.

## When to review the guide

Before finishing any frontend change, REVIEW whether the tour needs updating and
state your decision. Treat these as triggers:

- **New page / route added** (in `frontend/apps/web/src/app/App.js`) → a tour is
  REQUIRED.
- **UI change** — a targeted element was renamed, moved, restyled, or removed,
  or a `data-tour` / class selector changed → update the affected step's
  `selector`, `title`, or `body` (a broken selector silently falls back to a
  centered tooltip).
- **Function change** — a feature's behavior changed enough that the step's
  description is now misleading → update the `body`.
- **Navigation change** — a route path, redirect, or query param the tour relies
  on changed (e.g. `match`/selector assumptions) → update the `match` predicate
  and steps.

If a change doesn't affect any tour, say so explicitly (e.g. "no guide update
needed") rather than skipping the review silently.

## How to add / update a guide

1. **Register a tour** in `frontend/apps/web/src/constants/tours.js`:
   - Add an entry to `TOURS` with a unique `id`, a `match(pathname, search)`
     predicate for the route (use `search` for query-param-specific tours like
     `edit?tab=1`), and a `steps` array.
   - Each step needs a globally-unique `id` (prefix with the tour id, e.g.
     `deck.learn`), a `selector` pointing at a real element, a short `title`,
     and a helpful `body`.

2. **Mark the target elements** on the page with `data-tour="..."` attributes
   (preferred) so selectors stay stable. Existing unique class names may be used
   when adding markers isn't practical.

3. Nothing else is required — `TourProvider` (mounted in
   `frontend/apps/web/src/components/mainContainer.js`) auto-shows remaining steps on the
   first visit, and `GuideTour` renders the spotlight/arrow UI. Per-step
   completion is persisted via `frontend/apps/web/src/utils/tourStorage.js`.

Keep steps concise (1–3 sentences), point at the most important actions on the
page, and order them the way a new user would naturally use the page.
